</wbr><wbr><wbr </col><col><col </fingerprint><fingerprint><fingerprint </chart><chart><chart </usermedia><usermedia><usermedia </input><input><input </textarea><textarea><textarea </progress><progress><progress </meter><meter><meter </nav><nav><nav </h4><h4><h4 </h5><h5><h5 </h6><h6><h6 </span><span><span </div><div><div </aside><aside><aside </var><var><var </sub><sub><sub </sup><sup><sup </q><q><q </ruby><ruby><ruby </rt><rt><rt </bdi><bdi><bdi </bdo><bdo><bdo </pre><pre><pre </verse><verse><verse </colgroup><colgroup><colgroup </tfoot><tfoot><tfoot </zapp><zapp><zapp </picture><picture><picture </dialog><dialog><dialog </form><form><form </fieldset><fieldset><fieldset </legend><legend><legend </datalist><datalist><datalist </select><select><select </optgroup><optgroup><optgroup </button><button><button </output><output><output </footer><footer><footer </img><img><img </figure><figure><figure </figcaption><figcaption><figcaption </del><del><del </ins><ins><ins </dfn><dfn><dfn </kbd><kbd><kbd </label><label><label </option><option><option </table><table><table </caption><caption><caption </thead><thead><thead </tbody><tbody><tbody </dl><dl><dl </time><time><time </ol><ol><ol </details><details><details </summary><summary><summary </head><head><head </header><header><header </main><main><main </ztml><ztml><ztml </weft><weft><weft </body><body><body </h1><h1><h1 </samp><samp><samp </dt><dt><dt </dd><dd><dd </e><e><e </tr><tr><tr </th><th><th </td><td><td </blockquote><blockquote><blockquote </ul><ul><ul </article><article><article </claim><claim><claim </h2><h2><h2 </cite><cite><cite </abbr><abbr><abbr </mark><mark><mark </strong><strong><strong </em><em><em </code><code><code </a><a><a </li><li><li </h3><h3><h3 </section><section><section </p><p><p  dir=" start=" reversed=" colspan=" render=" of=" grants=" from=" x=" y=" into=" open=" step=" list=" selected=" placeholder=" low=" high=" optimum=" span=" kind=" for=" type=" min=" label=" lang=" alt=" size=" max=" src=" hash=" at=" name=" via=" version=" version-id=" signature=" key=" doc-id=" zs=" cite=" value=" title=" scope=" id=" ref=" href="@context {entity <claim subject: predicate: object: @warrant: @type @substrate: name: "description: "confidence: sources: [definitionempiricalconjectureaxiomattestationconsensusderivedschema:hist:weft:urn:uuid:urn:concept:urn:artifact:zdoc://sha256:ed25519:ed25519-pk:https://<https://schema.org/> as schema<https://vocab.weft.org/core/> as weft
  
    
      
        
          
            
              
                
<!-- A deliberately multilingual fixture, added before E4 begins.
     The rest of the corpus contains exactly three non-ASCII characters
     between all seven documents, so nothing in it reaches a right-to-left
     run, a complex-shaping face, or a font the system has to fall back to.
     Everything E4 builds - shaping, fallback, bidi, line breaking - would
     otherwise land with no coverage at all. -->
<ztml version="0.3" version-id="urn:uuid:eefc2a84-e905-4fa9-a20e-6b16d297afa1" signature="ed25519:QEUVKPb5hVelGDNkk+Ox0N2HjqucmjFH2K/guCpbT6faJdwliouB83uRmpxlZhQCUDeEcEfmXE/53BkgrWEhDw==" key="ed25519-pk:W+8vntKr49EShdQ7qXNm76+jrn3OPImAOmI5USWZde8=">
  <head doc-id="urn:uuid:5146a576-ab37-4c75-8505-4fde973bcf06" zs="/style.zss" title="One mechanism, many scripts"/>

  <weft>
    @context {
      <https://schema.org/> as schema
      <https://vocab.weft.org/core/> as weft
    }

    entity <zdoc://grazer.local/scripts.zdoc> &doc {
      @type schema:TextDigitalDocument
      @substrate: digital
      name: "One mechanism, many scripts"
    }

    entity <urn:artifact:antikythera> &mechanism {
      @type schema:Thing
      @substrate: physical
      name: "the Antikythera mechanism"
    }

    entity <urn:topic:writing-systems> &scripts {
      @type schema:Thing
      @substrate: abstract
      name: "writing systems"
    }

    claim c_about {
      subject: <zdoc://grazer.local/scripts.zdoc>
      predicate: schema:about
      object: <urn:topic:writing-systems>
      @warrant: axiom
    }

    claim c_named {
      subject: <urn:artifact:antikythera>
      predicate: schema:name
      object: "the Antikythera mechanism"
      @warrant: definition
    }

    claim c_shaping {
      subject: <urn:topic:writing-systems>
      predicate: schema:description
      object: "Some scripts cannot be rendered by mapping characters to glyphs in order."
      @warrant: consensus { support: 0.99 }
    }
  </weft>

  <body>
    <h1>One mechanism, many scripts</h1>

    <p>The <e ref="mechanism">Antikythera mechanism</e> is named after the
       island it sank beside. Written out, that name reaches most of the
       problems a text engine has:</p>

    <p>Greek, where it was found: to μηχανισμό των Αντικυθήρων.</p>

    <p>Arabic, which runs right to left and joins its letters:
       <em>آلة أنتيكيثيرا</em> — and a Latin word, Antikythera, set inside
       the same paragraph so the two directions have to be ordered against
       each other.</p>

    <p>Thai, which is written without spaces between words, so a line can
       only be broken by knowing the language: เครื่องกลอันติคีเทรา.</p>

    <p>Devanagari, where vowel signs are reordered and consonants combine:
       एंटीकाइथेरा यंत्र.</p>

    <p>Chinese, where every character is a possible line break:
       安提基特拉機械.</p>

    <p>And a sequence that is one thing to a reader and several codepoints
       to a program: 🇬🇷 ⚙️ 👩‍🔬 — a flag, a gear, and a scientist assembled
       from a woman, a joiner, and a microscope.</p>

    <p><claim ref="c_shaping">Some scripts cannot be rendered by mapping
       characters to glyphs in order.</claim> That is why shaping is a
       separate step from layout, and why it happens before line breaking
       rather than after.</p>
  </body>
</ztml>
<!-- painted.zdoc — the fixture E5 needed and the corpus did not have.
     Backgrounds, borders, radii, opacity and nested clip ports, on purpose.

     Every other document here is prose: `showcase.zdoc` emits 82 glyph runs
     and exactly TWO rects, one of which is the page background painted on an
     identical canvas and therefore invisible. So the whole rect half of the
     display list was exercised by one 3px border strip, 1553px down a page.

     `position.rs` already recorded this shape for positioning — "the corpus
     has nothing positioned, so every unit test would pass for a pass that
     never ran" — and E5.5 hit it again for backgrounds. This is the standing
     answer rather than a per-test sheet. -->
<ztml version="0.3" version-id="urn:uuid:eefc2a84-e905-4fa9-a20e-6b16d297afa1" signature="ed25519:eErELZkX6+T4Ig0lO1BhhQoxkLBqLr27Z6iWT/B4S3Y2jw2V6+wK8PQAv7cCb1IbWK6GXGCb7x7VMik8ppz7CQ==" key="ed25519-pk:W+8vntKr49EShdQ7qXNm76+jrn3OPImAOmI5USWZde8=">
  <head doc-id="urn:uuid:7c1f3a54-9b2e-4d61-8a03-5e2f7c9d1b48" zs="/painted.zss" title="painted surfaces"/>

  <weft>
    @context {
      <https://schema.org/> as schema
    }

    entity <zdoc://grazer.local/painted.zdoc> &doc {
      @type schema:TextDigitalDocument
      @substrate: digital
      name: "painted surfaces"
    }

    entity <urn:topic:paint> &topic {
      @type schema:Thing
      @substrate: abstract
      name: "the display list's rect half"
    }

    claim c1 {
      subject: <zdoc://grazer.local/painted.zdoc>
      predicate: schema:about
      object: <urn:topic:paint>
      @warrant: axiom
    }
  </weft>

  <body>
    <h1>Painted surfaces</h1>

    <p>Every block below carries a background, a border, or both, so the
       rect half of the display list has something to be wrong about.</p>

    <!-- Plain background, no border. -->
    <section id="plain">
      <p>A filled block with no border at all.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Background plus a wide border, to catch the two overlapping. -->
    <section id="bordered">
      <p>A filled block with a wide inline-start border. The border draws
         over the background, which is why they are emitted in that order.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Radius, which cascaded into nothing until E5.5. -->
    <section id="rounded">
      <p>Rounded corners. <code>border-radius</code> parsed and cascaded from
         the beginning and reached no consumer until the display list.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Opacity, which is a per-command alpha multiply and not CSS's group
         composite. Two children so the difference is visible. -->
    <section id="faded">
      <p>Half-transparent. The engine scales this command's alpha; CSS would
         composite the whole subtree as one group.</p>
      <p>A sibling, at full strength, because opacity does not inherit.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Nested clip ports: the case a flat sweep cannot express, and the
         reason paint became a tree walk in E5.7. -->
    <section id="outer-port">
      <p>Outer clip.</p>
      <section id="inner-port">
        <p>An inner clip inside the outer one. Both must find their own pop.</p>
      </section>
    </section>

    <!-- Inline backgrounds, which sit on `observed-box` rather than
         `world-box` — E4.27's third column. -->
    <p>Inline elements can be painted too:
       <mark>a highlighted run</mark>, and
       <code>a monospaced one</code>, inside ordinary prose.</p>
  </body>
</ztml>
<!-- A ZTML showcase. Read the source alongside the rendering: there is not one
     styling instruction anywhere in this file. No class, no style, no b, no i,
     no font, no align, no width — those attributes and elements do not exist
     in the language, so a document cannot express appearance even by accident.
     Everything you see is grazer's default rendering of the semantics. -->
<ztml version="0.3" version-id="urn:uuid:eefc2a84-e905-4fa9-a20e-6b16d297afa1" lang="en" signature="ed25519:/AHxHTLs1lilU6B18CGOZ63Z8ZuKPt1P1gBW9rZkb6Igg3uFP5QKRCpNxmqdZIWjcmm39aKzVUafJq2/r+jwDQ==" key="ed25519-pk:W+8vntKr49EShdQ7qXNm76+jrn3OPImAOmI5USWZde8=">

  <head doc-id="urn:uuid:f284c639-3d4f-4f38-ac20-9982d9905edf" zs="/style.zss" title="What ZTML Removes"/>

  <weft>
    @context {
      <https://schema.org/> as schema
      <https://vocab.weft.org/core/> as weft
      <https://vocab.example.org/history/> as hist
    }

    entity <zdoc://grazer.local/showcase.zdoc> &doc {
      @type schema:TechArticle
      @substrate: digital
      name: "What ZTML Removes"
    }

    entity <urn:concept:ztml> &ztml {
      @type schema:ComputerLanguage
      @substrate: abstract
      name: "ZTML"
      description: "Markup carrying structure and semantics, and nothing else."
    }

    entity <urn:artifact:antikythera> &mechanism {
      @type schema:Thing
      @substrate: physical
      name: "Antikythera Mechanism"
    }

    claim c_about {
      subject: <zdoc://grazer.local/showcase.zdoc>
      predicate: schema:about
      object: <urn:concept:ztml>
      @warrant: definition
    }

    claim c_separation {
      subject: <urn:concept:ztml>
      predicate: hist:enforces
      object: "structure and presentation are different documents"
      @warrant: axiom
    }

    claim c_failclosed {
      subject: <urn:concept:ztml>
      predicate: hist:parseStrategy
      object: "fail closed on the first violation"
      @warrant: definition
    }

    claim c_gears {
      subject: <urn:artifact:antikythera>
      predicate: hist:gearCount
      object: "at least 30 meshing gears"
      @warrant: empirical { confidence: 0.94, sources: [c_separation] }
    }

    claim c_adoption {
      subject: <urn:concept:ztml>
      predicate: hist:adoptionOutlook
      object: "authors will accept a closed vocabulary"
      @warrant: conjecture { confidence: 0.4 }
    }
  </weft>

  <body>
    <main>
      <header>
        <h1>What ZTML Removes</h1>
        <p>
          <claim ref="c_separation">Structure and presentation are separate
          documents</claim>, and this file is the demonstration: it contains no
          styling instruction of any kind, because the language has no way to
          express one.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>A figure, pinned by hash</h2>
        <figure>
          <img src="/gears.png"
               alt="Three meshing gears of decreasing size, drawn in blue, brown and green"
               hash="sha256:c4b2853cae028b8a54bd529bd72f15718c856c0542a4ea6f0dd0e37abf666736"
               size="720x360"/>
          <figcaption>
            <claim ref="c_gears">The mechanism carries at least thirty meshing
            gears.</claim> The image above is referenced by content hash, and
            arrived pushed on its own stream in the same round trip as this
            document.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          The <code>hash</code> attribute is mandatory, not advisory. A
          reference without one is a parse error, so subresource integrity is
          how references work rather than something an author opts into.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What is gone, and where it went</h2>
        <table>
          <caption>Removed from HTML, and its replacement</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Removed</th>
              <th scope="col">Why</th>
              <th scope="col">Now lives in</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">class</th>
              <td>a styling hook wearing a semantic costume</td>
              <td>ZSS selectors on real structure</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">style</th>
              <td>presentation inlined into content</td>
              <td>ZSS</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">b, i, font</th>
              <td>appearance with no meaning</td>
              <td>strong, em, or ZSS</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">script, on*</th>
              <td>ambient authority over the document</td>
              <td>ZAPPs, with declared grants</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">div soup</th>
              <td>structure with nothing to say</td>
              <td>section, article, nav, aside</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Semantics that survived</h2>
        <dl>
          <dt>abbr</dt>
          <dd>
            <abbr title="Zettelkasten Text Markup Language">ZTML</abbr> expands
            on demand, because an abbreviation is a fact about the word.
          </dd>
          <dt>time</dt>
          <dd>
            The wreck was found in <time at="1901">1901</time> — the machine
            form is the element, not an annotation bolted onto it.
          </dd>
          <dt>blockquote and cite</dt>
          <dd>Attribution is structural, not a visual convention.</dd>
        </dl>

        <blockquote cite="https://example.org/ztml/rationale">
          <p>
            HTML's permissive parsing is how it accumulated cruft.
            <claim ref="c_failclosed">ZTML refuses the first step</claim>.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Ordered and unordered</h2>
        <p>The parse pipeline, in order:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Lex, rejecting anything outside the closed vocabulary.</li>
          <li>Build the tree, rejecting malformed nesting.</li>
          <li>Validate the document shape: head, weft, body, in that order.</li>
          <li>Run the node gate over the embedded graph.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Things this document deliberately cannot do:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Set a colour, a font, or a width.</li>
          <li>Run code, or register an event handler.</li>
          <li>Reference a subresource without pinning its bytes.</li>
          <li>
            Claim its own authorship — <em>that</em> is rendered in the
            browser's chrome, from a signature, never from the page.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Progressive disclosure, without script</h2>
        <details>
          <summary>Why a closed vocabulary?</summary>
          <p>
            An open vocabulary means a parser must guess at anything it does
            not recognise, and guessing is how quirks mode was born. A closed
            one makes the unknown element a named error instead.
          </p>
        </details>
        <p>
          <claim ref="c_adoption">Whether authors will accept that trade is
          genuinely open</claim> — a conjecture at 0.4, and the browser renders
          it as one rather than letting the confident prose around it carry the
          reader past the uncertainty.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Onward</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="/antikythera.zdoc" via="schema:about">The mechanism
          itself</a>, or <a href="/shipwreck.zdoc" via="hist:recoveredFrom">the
          wreck it came from</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</ztml>
<!-- The christmas tree: every element ZTML's registry knows, in one document.
     Its job is coverage, not realism. `christmas_covers_every_registered_element`
     in tests/golden.rs fails when the registry grows and this does not, which is
     what keeps it honest — a fixture that claims to cover everything and is not
     checked is worth less than one that admits what it misses. -->
<ztml version="0.3" version-id="urn:uuid:eefc2a84-e905-4fa9-a20e-6b16d297afa1" signature="ed25519:jvnml3555AiJVtoeZUNOBv6dx+VXty8qlb4GYSrXF6Ns8BhwqUl0NxR95KiPXKmsAIYCYLuLMhMnTZYa5vkuAQ==" key="ed25519-pk:W+8vntKr49EShdQ7qXNm76+jrn3OPImAOmI5USWZde8=">
  <head doc-id="urn:uuid:7c1f0b52-9a3e-4d61-8f24-1b6d5e0a9c73" zs="/style.zss /christmas.zss" title="christmas tree"/>

  <weft>
    @context {
      <https://schema.org/> as schema
      <https://vocab.weft.org/core/> as weft
      <https://vocab.example.org/orchard/> as orch
    }

    entity <zdoc://grazer.local/christmas.zdoc> &doc {
      @type schema:TextDigitalDocument
      @substrate: digital
      name: "christmas tree"
    }

    entity <urn:orchard:spruce> &spruce {
      @type schema:Thing
      @substrate: physical
      name: "Picea abies"
      height: 21.4
      planted: 1974
    }

    entity <urn:orchard:survey> &survey {
      @type schema:Dataset
      @substrate: digital
      name: "orchard survey"
    }

    claim about {
      subject: <zdoc://grazer.local/christmas.zdoc>
      predicate: schema:about
      object: <urn:orchard:spruce>
      @warrant: axiom
    }

    claim measured {
      subject: <urn:orchard:spruce>
      predicate: orch:heightMetres
      object: 21.4
      @warrant: assertion { claimant: <did:key:z6MkSurveyor>, confidence: 0.93 }
    }

    claim guessed {
      subject: <urn:orchard:spruce>
      predicate: orch:ageYears
      object: 52
      @warrant: conjecture { confidence: 0.41 }
    }
  </weft>

  <body>
    <header>
      <h1>Every element, once</h1>
      <nav>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="zdoc://grazer.local/index.zdoc">Index</a></li>
          <li><a href="zdoc://grazer.local/showcase.zdoc" via="schema:relatedLink">Showcase</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <article>
        <h2>Sectioning and headings</h2>
        <h3>Third</h3>
        <h4>Fourth</h4>
        <h5>Fifth</h5>
        <h6>Sixth</h6>
        <p>A paragraph with <span id="inline-span">a span that declares a
           purpose</span> and <div id="inline-div">a div that declares
           one</div>.</p>

        <section id="clipped-port">
          <h3>A clip port</h3>
          <p>This section sets <code>overflow: hidden</code> and a fixed
             block size, so its content overflows and E4.16's
             <code>clipped</code> column has something true to report. Nothing
             else in the corpus establishes a scroll or clip port at all, so
             without this the column is all-false everywhere.</p>
          <p>A second paragraph, to be sure the port is overrun.</p>
          <p id="escapes-the-port">Inset past the port's own height, so an
             ancestor with a clip port genuinely does not contain it.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="stacking">
          <h3>Stacking</h3>
          <p id="beneath">Beneath, in flow.</p>
          <p id="floats-over">Out of flow, painted over.</p>
          <!-- A static wrapper between the positioned ancestor and the
               positioned box, so `containing-block` differs from `parent`
               and E4.23's walk has something to walk. Without this every
               out-of-flow box's containing block is simply its parent, and
               the column looks identical to `parent`. -->
          <section id="static-wrapper">
            <p id="pinned">Pinned to the containing block, not to the wrapper.</p>
          </section>
        </section>

        <aside id="an-aside">
          <p>An aside, which no other fixture has.</p>
        </aside>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h2>Text semantics</h2>
        <p><em>emphasis</em>, <strong>strong</strong>, <mark>marked</mark>,
           <code>code</code>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>, <samp>output</samp>,
           <var>x</var>, <cite>A Citation</cite>, H<sub>2</sub>O,
           2<sup>16</sup>, <abbr title="Zed Text Markup Language">ZTML</abbr>,
           <dfn>a definition</dfn>.</p>
        <p><del cite="zdoc://grazer.local/index.zdoc" at="2026-01-02">struck</del>
           <ins cite="zdoc://grazer.local/index.zdoc" at="2026-01-03">inserted</ins>
           <q cite="zdoc://grazer.local/index.zdoc">an inline quotation</q></p>
        <p><ruby>漢<rt>kan</rt></ruby> and
           <bdi>עברית</bdi> and
           <bdo dir="rtl">reversed</bdo> and a break<wbr/>opportunity.</p>
        <pre>  preformatted
  two lines</pre>
        <blockquote cite="zdoc://grazer.local/shipwreck.zdoc">
          <p>A block quotation.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <verse>A verse block,
line broken as written.</verse>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h2>Lists and tables</h2>
        <ol start="3" reversed="true">
          <li value="3">Third</li>
          <li>Second</li>
        </ol>
        <dl>
          <dt>Term</dt>
          <dd>Definition</dd>
        </dl>
        <table>
          <caption>A table with every part</caption>
          <colgroup><col span="2"/></colgroup>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Axis</th><th scope="col">Value</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>height</td><td>21.4</td></tr>
          </tbody>
          <tfoot>
            <tr><td colspan="2">One row measured</td></tr>
          </tfoot>
        </table>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h2>Graph-bearing and generated</h2>
        <p>The subject is <e ref="&spruce">a spruce</e>, and
           <claim ref="measured">its height is asserted at high
           confidence</claim>, while
           <claim ref="guessed">its age is only conjectured</claim>.</p>
        <p>Surveyed <time at="2026-03-14" render="relative"/>, over
           <time span="P3M"/>.</p>
        <p>Integrity: <fingerprint of="&doc"/></p>

        <!-- §5.4/§5.5/§5.6: `into=` and `from=` name a <zapp>, never a weft
             alias. The parser enforces it, which is how this document learned
             it needs a component before it can have a chart. -->
        <zapp src="/orchard.wasm"
              hash="sha256:1b3d5f7a9c1e3b5d7f0a2c4e6b8d0f2a4c6e8b0d2f4a6c8e0b2d4f6a8c0e2b4d"
              name="orchard"
              grants="none"
              title="Orchard survey viewer"/>
        <chart from="orchard" kind="line" x="planted" y="height"
               title="Growth"/>
        <usermedia kind="camera" into="orchard" title="Field camera"/>
        <figure>
          <img src="/gears.png" alt="Bronze gearing"
               hash="sha256:9f2c1e7a4b6d8f03a5c9e1b7d4f6082a3c5e7b9d1f3a5c7e9b1d3f5a7c9e1b3d"
               size="480x320"/>
          <figcaption>A figure with a caption.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <picture>
          <!-- Sized, so E4.29 has something with a real height to lose: a
               block inside an inline used to be dropped entirely, and an
               unsized image would give it a zero box either way. -->
          <img src="/gears.png" alt="Bronze gearing again"
               hash="sha256:9f2c1e7a4b6d8f03a5c9e1b7d4f6082a3c5e7b9d1f3a5c7e9b1d3f5a7c9e1b3d"
               size="600x200"/>
        </picture>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h2>Declarative interaction</h2>
        <details open="true">
          <summary>A disclosure</summary>
          <p>Its content.</p>
        </details>
        <dialog>
          <p>A dialog, closed.</p>
        </dialog>
        <form>
          <fieldset>
            <legend>Measurements</legend>
            <label for="h">Height</label>
            <input type="number" id="h" name="height" min="0" max="100" step="0.1"
                   value="21.4" list="common"/>
            <datalist id="common">
              <option value="21.4">measured</option>
            </datalist>
            <select name="species" label="Species, genus and epithet">
              <optgroup label="Picea">
                <option value="abies" selected="true">abies</option>
              </optgroup>
            </select>
            <label>Care notes
              <textarea name="notes" placeholder="Notes"/>
            </label>
            <button type="submit" name="save" value="1">Save</button>
            <output for="h">21.4</output>
            <progress value="7" max="10"/>
            <meter value="21.4" min="0" max="30" low="5" high="25"
                   optimum="20"/>
          </fieldset>
        </form>
      </article>
    </main>

    <footer>
      <p>The end of the tree.</p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</ztml>
<!-- A demonstration ZDOC. The subject is chosen because its scholarship has
     genuine epistemic texture: hard physical facts, dated estimates carrying
     real uncertainty, named scholarly positions, and open conjecture — all in
     one document. On the legacy web these render identically. Here they do
     not, because the warrant is part of the data. -->
<ztml version="0.3" version-id="urn:uuid:eefc2a84-e905-4fa9-a20e-6b16d297afa1" lang="en" signature="ed25519:gSsN4b+KD1G2eSzPeoQPgJed7JDXADC707oBCUjj4xsfyS5IvdgyFjiNfZ6BRzst2N40hulIQu8qNWYODaZ3DA==" key="ed25519-pk:W+8vntKr49EShdQ7qXNm76+jrn3OPImAOmI5USWZde8=">

  <head doc-id="urn:uuid:c8dfafae-46c7-4d16-b30c-4e0af1ca5cdc" zs="/style.zss" title="The Antikythera Mechanism"/>

  <weft>
    @context {
      <https://schema.org/> as schema
      <https://vocab.weft.org/core/> as weft
      <https://vocab.example.org/history/> as hist
    }

    // The page as a node in the graph (PROFILE.md §8.2.1).
    entity <zdoc://grazer.local/antikythera.zdoc> &doc {
      @type schema:ScholarlyArticle
      @substrate: digital
      name: "The Antikythera Mechanism"
    }

    entity <urn:artifact:antikythera> &mechanism {
      @type schema:Thing
      @substrate: physical
      name: "Antikythera Mechanism"
      description: "A geared bronze device recovered from a Roman-era shipwreck."
    }

    entity <urn:place:antikythera> &wreck {
      @type schema:Place
      @substrate: physical
      name: "Antikythera wreck site"
    }

    entity <did:key:z6MkPriceDerek> &price {
      @type schema:Person
      @substrate: physical
      name: "Derek J. de Solla Price"
    }

    entity <did:key:z6MkFreethAlex> &freeth {
      @type schema:Person
      @substrate: physical
      name: "Tony Freeth"
    }

    entity <urn:concept:lunar-anomaly> &anomaly {
      @type schema:Thing
      @substrate: abstract
      name: "First lunar anomaly"
    }

    // --- The page's connecting claim: what this document is about.
    claim c_about {
      subject: <zdoc://grazer.local/antikythera.zdoc>
      predicate: schema:about
      object: <urn:artifact:antikythera>
      @warrant: definition
    }

    // --- Axiom: a physical fact, not a confident belief. No number attaches.
    claim c_recovered {
      subject: <urn:artifact:antikythera>
      predicate: hist:recoveredFrom
      object: <urn:place:antikythera>
      @warrant: axiom
    }

    // --- Definition: true by what the words mean.
    claim c_geared {
      subject: <urn:artifact:antikythera>
      predicate: hist:mechanismClass
      object: "geared analogue computer"
      @warrant: definition
    }

    // --- Assertion: a named person's position, with their confidence.
    //     The claimant travels with the claim when it is cited or extracted.
    claim c_price_gearing {
      subject: <urn:artifact:antikythera>
      predicate: hist:proposedGearTrain
      object: <did:key:z6MkPriceDerek>
      @warrant: assertion { claimant: <did:key:z6MkPriceDerek>, confidence: 0.90 }
      strength: 0.8
    }

    // --- Empirical: measured, graded, and it names the claims it rests on.
    claim c_dating {
      subject: <urn:artifact:antikythera>
      predicate: hist:constructionDate
      object: "c. 150-100 BCE"
      @warrant: empirical { confidence: 0.82, sources: [c_recovered] }
    }

    // --- Consensus: broad agreement, tracked separately from evidence.
    claim c_lunar {
      subject: <urn:artifact:antikythera>
      predicate: hist:models
      object: <urn:concept:lunar-anomaly>
      @warrant: consensus { support: 0.95 }
    }

    // --- Conjecture: openly a guess. No amount of confidence promotes this
    //     to a fact — that is the point of a typed warrant.
    claim c_missing {
      subject: <urn:artifact:antikythera>
      predicate: hist:mayHaveDisplayed
      object: "planetary positions on the lost front dial"
      @warrant: conjecture { confidence: 0.55 }
    }

    // --- Derived: a conclusion chained from premises, by a named rule.
    claim c_sophistication {
      subject: <urn:artifact:antikythera>
      predicate: hist:impliesTradition
      object: "an established Hellenistic gear-making practice"
      @warrant: derived {
        rule: "no artefact of this complexity is a first attempt",
        premises: [c_geared, c_dating]
      }
    }

    // --- Attestation: a claim ABOUT another claim. The subject is c_dating.
    claim c_freeth_attests {
      subject: c_dating
      predicate: weft:attestedBy
      object: <did:key:z6MkFreethAlex>
      @warrant: assertion { claimant: <did:key:z6MkFreethAlex>, confidence: 0.88 }
    }
  </weft>

  <body>
    <main>
      <header>
        <h1>The Antikythera Mechanism</h1>
        <p>
          Every sentence below is bound to a claim in this document's graph.
          The browser knows which are
          <claim ref="c_recovered">established fact</claim>,
          which are <claim ref="c_dating">measured estimates</claim>,
          and which are <claim ref="c_missing">frank conjecture</claim> —
          and it will not let this page pretend otherwise.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>What it is</h2>
        <p>
          The <e ref="mechanism">Antikythera Mechanism</e> is a
          <claim ref="c_geared">geared analogue computer</claim> of Hellenistic
          manufacture. It was <claim ref="c_recovered">recovered from a
          shipwreck off <e ref="wreck">the island of Antikythera</e></claim> by
          sponge divers, and survives as 82 corroded bronze fragments.
        </p>
        <p>
          Its density of engineering is startling: at least thirty meshing
          gears, cut by hand, carrying a model of the sky.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/shipwreck.zdoc" via="hist:recoveredFrom">More on the wreck
          it came from</a> — the <code>via</code> names the relation this link
          stands for, making the hyperlink a typed edge rather than an untyped
          jump.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>When it was made</h2>
        <p>
          Dating rests on inscription letterforms and on the wreck's cargo.
          The current estimate is
          <claim ref="c_dating">roughly 150 to 100 BCE</claim> — an empirical
          claim, carrying a confidence, resting on evidence this document
          names rather than gestures at.
        </p>
        <p>
          <claim ref="c_freeth_attests"><e ref="freeth">Tony Freeth</e>
          attests to that dating.</claim> Note what this is: not a louder
          restatement, but a separate claim <em>about</em> the first one, with
          its own author and its own signature.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What it computed</h2>
        <p>
          <claim ref="c_lunar">It models the first lunar anomaly</claim> — the
          Moon's variable speed through the sky — using a pin-and-slot device
          that produces a varying rate from two gears turning at a constant
          one. There is broad scholarly consensus on this.
        </p>
        <p>
          <claim ref="c_price_gearing"><e ref="price">Derek de Solla
          Price</e> proposed the first full gear train</claim> in 1974, an
          assertion carried under his name.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What we do not know</h2>
        <blockquote cite="https://example.org/antikythera/front-dial">
          <p>
            <claim ref="c_missing">The lost front dial may have displayed
            planetary positions.</claim>
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          This is a <strong>conjecture</strong>, and grazer renders it as one.
          It carries a confidence of 0.55, and no threshold anywhere promotes
          it to a fact: <code>conjecture</code> and <code>axiom</code> are
          different kinds, not different amounts.
        </p>
        <p>
          <claim ref="c_sophistication">That such a device existed at all
          implies an established tradition of gear-making</claim> — a derived
          claim, chained from the two premises it names.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Why this document is different</h2>
        <p>
          A conventional page asserts all of the above in the same voice. A
          reader cannot tell the fact from the estimate from the guess without
          leaving the page. Here the distinction is
          <em>in the data</em>, the browser enforces it, and the badge in the
          chrome reports what was actually checked — not "true", but
          <em>signed, hash-verified, and confidence on record</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Alter one character of one claim and its content hash changes
          completely, the document hash changes with it, and the visual
          fingerprint becomes unmistakably different. Tamper-evidence is not a
          feature bolted on; it falls out of addressing claims by their hash.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</ztml>
<!-- handsome.zdoc — a document that looks like something, without containing a
     single instruction about how it should look.

     Read this source next to the rendering. There is no class, no style, no
     colour, no font, no width anywhere in it, because ZTML has no way to
     express any of those. Everything the page does visually comes from
     handsome.zss selecting on structure that is here for other reasons —
     scope=row because it is a row header, warrant=conjecture because the
     claim really is a guess.

     The paper is dark. That is the sheet's decision, and grazer's contrast
     floor holds the sheet to it: every colour was authored against the dark
     paper, and the one that was not gets corrected at render time. -->
<ztml version="0.3" version-id="urn:uuid:eefc2a84-e905-4fa9-a20e-6b16d297afa1" lang="en" signature="ed25519:sMF4N5W4+eYh4tUZxc7UqDBH+3IRUArO+thTClGqg1ltaxmXtf2iKRreXhP5/ouOK/siUzqNbLBSCsikzzUZAQ==" key="ed25519-pk:W+8vntKr49EShdQ7qXNm76+jrn3OPImAOmI5USWZde8=">

  <head doc-id="urn:uuid:662719a7-a596-49e9-80ea-0fe3444d9242" zs="/handsome.zss" title="The Colour of a Claim"/>

  <weft>
    @context {
      <https://schema.org/> as schema
      <https://vocab.weft.org/core/> as weft
      <https://vocab.example.org/typography/> as typo
    }

    entity <zdoc://grazer.local/handsome.zdoc> &doc {
      @type schema:TechArticle
      @substrate: digital
      name: "The Colour of a Claim"
    }

    entity <urn:concept:zss> &zss {
      @type schema:ComputerLanguage
      @substrate: abstract
      name: "ZSS"
      description: "A style language that selects on meaning, including how a statement is warranted."
    }

    entity <urn:person:ottosson> &ottosson {
      @type schema:Person
      @substrate: physical
      name: "Björn Ottosson"
    }

    entity <urn:place:oklab> &oklab {
      @type schema:Place
      @substrate: abstract
      name: "OKLab"
    }

    entity <urn:org:grazer-spec> &spec {
      @type schema:Organization
      @substrate: abstract
      name: "SPEC-ZSS"
    }

    claim c_about {
      subject: <zdoc://grazer.local/handsome.zdoc>
      predicate: schema:about
      object: <urn:concept:zss>
      @warrant: definition
    }

    claim c_separation {
      subject: <urn:concept:zss>
      predicate: typo:enforces
      object: "presentation is a separate document from structure"
      @warrant: axiom
    }

    claim c_closed {
      subject: <urn:concept:zss>
      predicate: typo:parseStrategy
      object: "a sheet parses entirely or it does not apply"
      @warrant: definition
    }

    claim c_oklch {
      subject: <urn:person:ottosson>
      predicate: typo:published
      object: "OKLab, a perceptual colour space with even lightness"
      @warrant: empirical { confidence: 0.98 }
    }

    claim c_floor {
      subject: <urn:concept:zss>
      predicate: typo:enforces
      object: "computed text below the contrast floor is corrected, not rejected"
      @warrant: definition
    }

    claim c_red {
      subject: <urn:concept:zss>
      predicate: typo:observes
      object: "saturated red carries too little luminance to pass on dark paper"
      @warrant: empirical { confidence: 0.72, sources: [c_oklch] }
    }

    claim c_authors {
      subject: <urn:concept:zss>
      predicate: typo:adoptionOutlook
      object: "authors will prefer selecting on meaning to inventing class names"
      @warrant: conjecture { confidence: 0.45 }
    }

    claim c_dark {
      subject: <urn:concept:zss>
      predicate: typo:observes
      object: "a document is a surface, and a surface has a colour of its own"
      @warrant: assertion { claimant: <urn:org:grazer-spec>, confidence: 0.6 }
    }
  </weft>

  <body>
    <main>
      <header>
        <h1>The Colour of a Claim</h1>
        <p>
          A style sheet that can ask how a sentence is warranted, and a
          browser that will not let it make the answer unreadable.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section id="paper">
        <h2>The paper is part of the argument</h2>
        <p>
          <claim ref="c_dark">A document is a surface, and a surface has a
          colour of its own</claim> — so this sheet paints one. The
          declaration is a single <code>background-color</code> on
          <code>body</code>, and it does two jobs at once: it is what the
          window is painted with, and it is the datum the contrast floor
          measures every run against.
        </p>
        <p>
          Those two have to be the same colour. When they are not, the floor
          certifies text as legible on a surface nobody draws, and the reader
          gets dark ink on a dark window together with a clean bill of health.
        </p>

        <blockquote cite="https://example.org/zss/rationale">
          <p>
            <claim ref="c_floor">Computed text below the floor is corrected
            rather than rejected.</claim> A sheet with one dim rule is not a
            malformed sheet, and refusing the document over it would make the
            floor an obstacle authors route around instead of a guarantee
            readers get.
          </p>
          <cite>SPEC-ZSS §4</cite>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <section id="warrants">
        <h2>Prose, coloured by how it is known</h2>
        <p>
          Each sentence below is bound to a claim in this document's graph, and
          the sheet selects on the warrant rather than on anything the prose
          declares about itself.
        </p>

        <dl>
          <dt>Axiom</dt>
          <dd>
            <claim ref="c_separation">Presentation is a separate document from
            structure.</claim> Taken as given; the language is built on it.
          </dd>

          <dt>Definition</dt>
          <dd>
            <claim ref="c_closed">A sheet parses entirely or it does not
            apply.</claim> True because of what the words mean.
          </dd>

          <dt>Empirical</dt>
          <dd>
            <claim ref="c_oklch">Björn Ottosson published OKLab, a perceptual
            colour space with even lightness.</claim> Checkable, and checked.
          </dd>

          <dt>Assertion</dt>
          <dd>
            Someone said it, and the browser says who rather than pretending
            the page is a neutral narrator.
          </dd>

          <dt>Conjecture</dt>
          <dd>
            <claim ref="c_authors">Authors will prefer selecting on meaning to
            inventing class names.</claim> A guess at 0.45, rendered as one —
            faded and slanted, so the confident prose around it does not carry
            you past the uncertainty.
          </dd>
        </dl>

        <h3>Entities, by type and substrate</h3>
        <p>
          The graph also types its subjects, and the sheet can reach that too:
          <e ref="ottosson">Björn Ottosson</e> is a person and a physical one;
          <e ref="oklab">OKLab</e> is a place only in the sense that a colour
          space is somewhere you can be. <e ref="zss">ZSS</e> is abstract, and
          renders as such.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="red">
        <h2>Why the red had to move</h2>
        <p>
          <claim ref="c_red">Saturated red carries too little luminance to
          clear the floor on dark paper.</claim> A red that looks vivid on
          white lands near <samp>Lc 47</samp> here. The fix was not to abandon
          it but to raise its lightness until the author met the floor instead
          of the browser:
        </p>

        <table>
          <caption>The palette, measured against this document's own paper</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Role</th>
              <th scope="col">OKLCH</th>
              <th scope="col">Lc</th>
              <th scope="col">Verdict</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">ink</th>
              <td><code>oklch(93% 0.015 320)</code></td>
              <td><samp>91.9</samp></td>
              <td>clears comfortably</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">rose</th>
              <td><code>oklch(82% 0.14 25)</code></td>
              <td><samp>63.8</samp></td>
              <td>clears, after lifting</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">purple</th>
              <td><code>oklch(82% 0.14 310)</code></td>
              <td><samp>67.2</samp></td>
              <td>clears</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">gold</th>
              <td><code>oklch(84% 0.16 85)</code></td>
              <td><samp>73.8</samp></td>
              <td>clears easily</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">the first red</th>
              <td><del>oklch(72% 0.20 20)</del> <ins>oklch(82% 0.14 25)</ins></td>
              <td><samp>47.2</samp></td>
              <td></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          The last row's verdict cell is empty on purpose — an empty cell reads
          as deliberately empty rather than as broken, because
          <code>td:empty</code> is a thing a sheet can say.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="mechanics">
        <h2>What the sheet is actually doing</h2>

        <h3>Selecting on position</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>The standfirst is <code>h1 + p</code> — a paragraph is a
              standfirst because of where it sits.</li>
          <li>Table striping is <code>tr:nth-child(odd)</code>, so the markup
              never learns it is being striped.</li>
          <li>Row headers are <code>th[scope=row]</code>, styled off a fact
              about the table.</li>
          <li>This item is last, and knows it only via
              <code>li:last-child</code>.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Selecting on the graph</h3>
        <p>
          <dfn>Graph-facet selection</dfn> is the part with no CSS equivalent.
          <abbr title="Zettelkasten Style Sheets">ZSS</abbr> reaches through a
          bound element into the claim graph, so
          <code>claim[confidence&lt;0.5]</code> is a legal selector and
          <code>claim[warrant&gt;=axiom]</code> is a named error rather than a
          silently useless match.
        </p>

        <details>
          <summary>Why hue is never the only signal</summary>
          <p>
            Every warrant above carries weight, slope or an underline as well
            as a colour. A reader who does not separate these hues, or who
            prints the page, still sees the distinction. Colour is the second
            signal, never the first.
          </p>
        </details>

        <p>
          Press <kbd>Cmd</kbd> and follow <a href="/showcase.zdoc"
          via="schema:about">what ZTML removes</a>, or read
          <a href="/antikythera.zdoc" via="schema:about">the mechanism</a>.
          A typed edge renders differently from a bare link because
          <code>a[via]</code> says the relationship is declared.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="colophon">
        <h2>Colophon</h2>
        <p id="colophon-note">
          This paragraph is set in a purple at <samp>Lc 14.6</samp> against
          this paper, which is far below the floor. It is here on purpose.
          grazer lifts it along OKLCH lightness — same hue, same chroma — and
          reports the correction in the chrome, because a floor you cannot see
          working is indistinguishable from one that was never implemented.
          Set on <time at="2026-07-31">31 July 2026</time>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</ztml>
